<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Bremen-Verden</h1><p class="st-meta">1648 CE – 1712 CE · capital Stade</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Two secularised prince-bishoprics between the Elbe and Weser that Sweden ruled as duchies from 1648 until losing them to Denmark and Hanover.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 turned the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-archbishopric-of-bremen">Prince-Archbishopric of Bremen</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-verden">Prince-Bishopric of Verden</a> into secular duchies and granted them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> as imperial fiefs. The Swedish crown thus gained a seat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> and control of the mouths of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> governed the duchies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stade">Stade</a>, which it fortified, through a governor-general and the local estates. The city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Bremen</a> claimed to be a free imperial city, and Sweden fought two wars against it in 1654 and 1666 without subduing it. The lands were mainly marsh and heath farming country with a Lutheran population.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> occupied the duchies in 1712. Denmark sold them in 1715 to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-hanover">Electorate of Hanover</a>, whose elector was also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-i-of-great-britain">George I of Great Britain</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> ceded them formally in 1719. They later formed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> and of the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bremen-verden.jpg" alt="Bremen-Verden" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lower_Saxony_Landschaft_der_Herzogt%C3%BCmer_Bremen_und_Verden.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1648 CE – 1712 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Stade</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Christina, Charles XI</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German (Low German)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchies of Bremen and Verden, Hertigdömena Bremen och Verden</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1680&amp;focus=bremen-verden" aria-label="Bremen-Verden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4891 336 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bremen-Verden lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5211 592L5381 440M5211 592L5229 694M5211 592L5274 535"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5381 440h0M5229 694h0M5274 535h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5211 592h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5211 592h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Stade. <a href="/atlas?year=1680&amp;focus=bremen-verden">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

